@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Score and replay") {
        In this part, we'll add the score, music playback, and the ability to
        restart the game.

        We have to keep track of the current score in a variable and display
        it on screen using a minimal interface. We will use a text label to
        do that.
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating the score label") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            In the main scene, add a new child node
            [Control](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcontrol.html#class-control)
            to `Main` and name it `UserInterface`. Ensure you are on the 2D
            screen, where you can edit your User Interface (UI).

            The `UserInterface` node allows us to group our UI in a branch of
            the scene tree and use a theme resource that will propagate to all
            its children. We'll use it to set our game's font.

            @Image(source: 09-01.score-label-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a child node
                [Control](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcontrol.html#class-control)
                to `Main` and name it `UserInterface`.

                @Image(source: 09-02.add-user-interface-control.png, alt: "The UserInterface Control node added to Main")
            }
            @Step {
                Add a
                [Label](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Flabel.html#class-label)
                node and name it `ScoreLabel`.

                @Image(source: 09-03.add-score-label.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, set the _Label_'s _Text_ to a placeholder
                like "Score: 0".

                @Image(source: 09-04.score-placeholder-text.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Also, the text is white by default, like our game's
                background. We need to change its color to see it at runtime.

                Scroll down to _Theme Overrides_, and expand _Colors_ and
                enable _Font Color_ in order to tint the text to black (which
                contrasts well with the white 3D scene).

                > Tip: If you want a quick visual sanity check, set the
                > color to something loud (red or green) while you build
                > the layout, then dial it back to a more sensible color
                > when you're done.

                @Image(source: 09-05.score-font-color-black.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, tap and drag on the text in the viewport to move it
                away from the top-left corner.

                @Image(source: 09-06.score-label-positioned.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating a UI theme") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll create a theme resource on the `UserInterface` node that
            defines the default font and font size for the whole UI tree.

            > See also: You can add more properties to the theme resource to
            > design complex user interfaces, but that is beyond the scope of
            > this series. To learn more about creating and editing themes, see
            > [Introduction to GUI skinning](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/ui/gui%5Fskinning.html#doc-gui-skinning).

            @Image(source: 09-07.ui-theme-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Once again, select the `UserInterface` node. In the
                _Inspector_, create a new theme resource in _Theme -> Theme_.

                @Image(source: 09-08.create-theme-resource.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap on it to open the theme editor in the bottom panel. It
                gives you a preview of how all the built-in UI widgets will
                look with your theme resource.

                By default, a theme only has a few properties: _Default Base
                Scale_, _Default Font_ and _Default Font Size_.

                @Image(source: 09-09.theme-preview-panel.png)
            }
            @Step {
                The _Default Font_ expects a font file like the ones you have
                on your computer. Two common font file formats are TrueType
                Font (TTF) and OpenType Font (OTF).

                In the _File Tree_, expand the `fonts` directory and
                tap and drag the `Montserrat-Medium.ttf` file we included in
                the project onto the _Default Font_. The text will reappear
                in the theme preview.

                The text is a bit small. Set the _Default Font Size_ to `22`
                pixels to increase the text's size.

                @Image(source: 09-10.font-montserrat-size-22.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Keeping track of the score") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Let's work on the score next. Attach a new script to the
            `ScoreLabel` and define the `score` variable.

            The score should increase by `1` every time we squash a monster.
            We can use their `squashed` signal to know when that happens.
            However, because we instantiate monsters from the code, we
            cannot connect the mob signal to the `ScoreLabel` via the
            editor. Instead, we have to make the connection from the code
            every time we spawn a monster.

            The second line in `_on_mob_squashed()` uses the value of the
            `score` variable to replace the placeholder `%s`. When using
            this feature, Xogot automatically converts values to string
            text, which is convenient when outputting text in labels or when
            using the `print()` function.

            > See also: You can learn more about string formatting here:
            > [GDScript format strings](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript%5Fformat%5Fstring.html#doc-gdscript-printf).

            > Note: In a complex game, you may want to completely separate
            > your user interface from the game world. In that case, you
            > would not keep track of the score on the label. Instead, you
            > may want to store it in a separate, dedicated object. But
            > when prototyping or when your project is simple, it is fine
            > to keep your code simple. Programming is always a balancing
            > act.

            @Image(source: 09-11.score-tracking-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Attach a new script to the `ScoreLabel`.

                @Code(name: "score_label.gd", file: score_label-3d-00.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Define the `score` variable.

                @Code(name: "score_label.gd", file: score_label-3d-01.gd, previousFile: score_label-3d-00.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Open the script `main.gd`. If it's still open, you can tap
                on its name in the script editor's left column.

                Alternatively, you can double-tap the `main.gd` file in the
                _File Tree_.

                At the bottom of the `_on_mob_timer_timeout()` function, add
                the line that connects the mob's `squashed` signal to the
                `ScoreLabel`'s callback.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-05.gd, previousFile: main-3d-04.gd)
            }
            This line means that when the mob emits the `squashed` signal,
            the `ScoreLabel` node will receive it and call the function
            `_on_mob_squashed()`.

            @Step {
                Head back to the `score_label.gd` script to define the
                `_on_mob_squashed()` callback function. There, we increment
                the score and update the displayed text.

                @Code(name: "score_label.gd", file: score_label-3d-02.gd, previousFile: score_label-3d-01.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                You can now play the game and squash a few enemies to see
                the score increase.

                @Image(source: 09-12.score-displayed-in-game.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Retrying the game") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll now add the ability to play again after dying. When the
            player dies, we'll display a message on the screen and wait for
            input.

            @Image(source: 09-13.retry-overlay-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Head back to the `main.tscn` scene, select the
                `UserInterface` node, add a child node
                [ColorRect](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcolorrect.html#class-colorrect),
                and name it `Retry`. This node fills a rectangle with a
                uniform color and will serve as an overlay to darken the
                screen.

                @Image(source: 09-14.add-color-rect-retry.png)
            }
            @Step {
                To make it span over the whole viewport, you can use the
                _Anchor Preset_ menu in the toolbar. Open it and apply the
                _Full Rect_ command.

                Nothing happens. Well, almost nothing; only the four green
                pins move to the corners of the selection box.

                This is because UI nodes (all the ones with a green icon)
                work with anchors and margins relative to their parent's
                bounding box. Here, the `UserInterface` node has a small
                size and the `Retry` one is limited by it.

                @Image(source: 09-15.full-rect-anchor-preset.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `UserInterface` and apply _Anchor Preset -> Full
                Rect_ to it as well. The `Retry` node should now span the
                whole viewport.

                @Image(source: 09-16.retry-full-viewport.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's change its color so it darkens the game area. Select
                `Retry` and in the _Inspector_, set its _Color_ to
                something both dark and transparent. To do so, in the color
                picker, drag the _A_ slider to the left. It controls the
                color's Alpha channel, that is to say, its
                opacity/transparency.

                > Tip: A black rectangle at 50% alpha (Color(0, 0, 0, 0.5))
                > gives a classic "modal" feel — the player can still see
                > the world behind the overlay, but it's clearly dimmed.

                @Image(source: 09-17.retry-color-transparent.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Next, add a
                [Label](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Flabel.html#class-label)
                as a child of `Retry` and give it the _Text_ "Press Enter to
                retry." To move it and anchor it in the center of the
                screen, apply _Anchor Preset -> Center_ to it.

                @Image(source: 09-18.retry-label-centered.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the retry option") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We can now head to the code to show and hide the `Retry` node
            when the player dies and plays again.

            @Image(source: 09-19.retry-code-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the script `main.gd`. First, we want to hide the
                overlay at the start of the game. Add this line to the
                `_ready()` function.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-06.gd, previousFile: main-3d-05.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Then, when the player gets hit, we show the overlay.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-07.gd, previousFile: main-3d-06.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, when the `Retry` node is visible, we need to
                listen to the player's input and restart the game if they
                press enter. To do this, we use the built-in
                `_unhandled_input()` callback, which is triggered on any
                input.

                If the player pressed the predefined `ui_accept` input
                action and `Retry` is visible, we reload the current
                scene.

                > Note: `ui_accept` is a built-in action mapped to Enter
                > and gamepad A by default, so we don't need to add it to
                > our Input Map.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-08.gd, previousFile: main-3d-07.gd)
            }
            The function `get_tree()` gives us access to the global
            [SceneTree](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fscenetree.html#class-scenetree)
            object, which allows us to reload and restart the current
            scene.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding music") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            To add music that plays continuously in the background, we're
            going to use another feature in Xogot:
            [autoloads](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/singletons%5Fautoload.html#doc-singletons-autoload).

            To play audio, all you need to do is add an
            [AudioStreamPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Faudiostreamplayer.html#class-audiostreamplayer)
            node to your scene and attach an audio file to it. When you
            start the scene, it can play automatically. However, when you
            reload the scene, like we do to play again, the audio nodes are
            also reset, and the music starts back from the beginning.

            You can use the autoload feature to have Xogot load a node or a
            scene automatically at the start of the game, outside the
            current scene. You can also use it to create globally
            accessible objects.

            Before we wrap up this lesson, here's a quick look at how it
            works under the hood. When you run the game, your _Scene Tree_
            changes to give you two tabs: _Remote_ and _Local_.

            The _Remote_ tab allows you to visualize the node tree of your
            running game. There, you will see the _Main_ node and
            everything the scene contains and the instantiated mobs at the
            bottom.

            At the top are the autoloaded `MusicPlayer` and a _root_ node,
            which is your game's viewport.

            @Image(source: 09-20.music-autoload-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a new scene by opening the **Document Menu** (the down-arrow
                icon) in the upper-left corner and tapping **New Scene**.

                @Image(source: 09-21.new-scene-music-player.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap the _Other Node_ button to create an
                [AudioStreamPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Faudiostreamplayer.html#class-audiostreamplayer)
                and rename it to `MusicPlayer`.

                @Image(source: 09-22.audio-stream-player-node.png)
            }
            @Step {
                We included a music soundtrack in the `art/` directory,
                `House In a Forest Loop.ogg`. Tap and drag it onto the
                _Stream_ property in the _Inspector_. Also, turn on
                _Autoplay_ so the music plays automatically at the start of
                the game.

                > Tip: Toggle off _Autoplay_ here if you want to start the
                > music from a script later (e.g. fade in over the first
                > few seconds). Otherwise, leave it on and the music
                > starts as soon as the scene loads.

                @Image(source: 09-23.music-stream-autoplay.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene as `music_player.tscn`.

                We have to register it as an autoload. Tap the **"..."** toolbar
                button in the upper-right corner, choose **Settings**, and then tap
                the **Dropdown Menu** next to **General** to select **Globals -> Autoload**.

                In the _Path_ field, you want to enter the path to your
                scene. Tap the folder icon to open the file browser and
                double-tap on `music_player.tscn`. Then, tap the _Add_
                button on the right to register the node.

                > Important: When registering the autoload, the _Node
                > Name_ defaults to the file's name (`music_player`).
                > Leave it as is — that's the name we'll use to reference
                > the autoload from any script.

                @Image(source: 09-24.register-autoload-settings.png)
            }
            @Step {
                `music_player.tscn` now loads into any scene you open or
                play. So if you run the game now, the music will play
                automatically in any scene.

                @Image(source: 09-25.scene-dock-remote-local.png)
            }
            And that does it for this lesson. In the next part, we'll add
            an animation to make the game both look and feel much nicer.

            Here is the complete `main.gd` script for reference.

            @Step {
                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-08.gd, reset: true)
            }
        }
    }
}
